{
  "evaluation": "In Study B, the claim explicitly notes that the 15D depression dimension score \"significantly increased (improved) from baseline.\" Because the normalized outcome assumes \"lower is better,\" this increase was mislabeled as \"harm,\" when on a close read both studies actually found a benefit. In addition, Study B measures depression as one dimension of a wider health-related quality-of-life instrument (15D) against Study A's full depression scale (HAMD), which fits the rule's exact example for an `apparent` disagreement with reason `outcome_measure` (\"mood rated as one item of a wider symptom checklist against a full depression scale\"). Responses B and C both correctly identify the `apparent` verdict, the `outcome_measure` reason, and the appropriate `also_differs` fields. Response B is ranked ahead of C for its more thorough rationale and detail explaining both the scale polarity inversion and the single-item QoL dimension versus full scale distinction. Response A misses the scale polarity/extraction issue entirely and mistakenly labels the pair `genuine`.",
  "ranking": [
    "B",
    "C",
    "A"
  ]
}
